1
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:23,440
Today we are going to jump into a clinical subject and discuss patient assessment in

2
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:28,100
critical care transport. The background on this is that we've gotten the question a handful of

3
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:32,400
times from our nursing friends, how do you know what to do when you get a sick patient?

4
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:38,560
EMS folks across the board are pretty good at managing the initial steps in assessing and

5
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:43,340
treating critically ill or injured patients due to the specific way we teach and practice patient

6
00:01:43,340 --> 00:01:49,400
assessment. This is somewhat different than the way nurses are taught assessment. EMS offers an

7
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:53,680
algorithmic approach to patient assessment that is fairly consistent across different programs

8
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:58,220
and institutions. It guides clinicians in the initial approach to both trauma and medical

9
00:01:58,220 --> 00:02:04,040
patients presenting on scene with acute injuries or illness. The nursing field, on the other hand,

10
00:02:04,580 --> 00:02:09,200
is a bit less consistent in its approach to patient assessment and tends to teach it via

11
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:14,780
a collection of different components, one of which is the systems-based approach. That said,

12
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:20,120
this method of organizing a patient assessment and some of the specific components do have value

13
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:25,920
in the CCT world. The general idea with this episode is to explain how patient assessment

14
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:30,860
in EMS works for those who are unfamiliar with it, and then to do the same for one of the ways

15
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:36,400
assessment is taught in nursing. Both approaches have value, and the end result is a sort of hybrid

16
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:41,500
that works well in the critical care transport setting. We'll also mention some strategies for

17
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:45,500
practice and betterment so that listeners can dial in their assessment techniques.

18
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:58,400
The foundation of patient assessment in EMS comes from a template used by the National Registry of EMTs to evaluate EMS providers during a psychomotor assessment for initial certification.

19
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:09,380
The way it works is that the NREMT has a skill sheet that proctors use to verify competency as EMS students move through the assessment and management of patients in simulated scenarios.

20
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:16,280
They basically mark off line items in a checklist, and a score is determined by how many of those things are performed correctly.

21
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:24,140
Now, this approach, as both a learning tool and a method for the evaluation of entry-level providers, has come under scrutiny,

22
00:03:24,680 --> 00:03:30,720
but the point still stands that the vast majority of EMS programs utilize this framework to teach patient assessment.

23
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:39,160
And the result of this is that most EMS providers use, or were at least taught during initial training, a similar sequence of steps for patient assessment.

24
00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:44,980
It's probably easier to appreciate how this works in a video format, so we'll add some

25
00:03:44,980 --> 00:03:46,840
links in the show notes for those who are interested.

26
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:49,700
But here's our audio version of how it all works.

27
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:55,620
We'll start with an unresponsive trauma assessment, and then can discuss the differences for medical

28
00:03:55,620 --> 00:03:58,620
patients and responsive trauma patients in just a second.

29
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:04,780
The first step is the scene size up or scene survey, a series of steps that happens even

30
00:04:04,780 --> 00:04:06,280
before patient contact is made.

31
00:04:07,220 --> 00:04:11,440
Scene safety and BSI is the initial catchphrase most often heard.

32
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:20,980
It includes ensuring that the scene is safe for EMS providers to operate in, and then utilizing body substance isolation, BSI, techniques to protect oneself.

33
00:04:21,740 --> 00:04:26,620
This essentially means putting on gloves, but also considering other PPE as appropriate.

34
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:30,900
Next, we consider mechanism of injury or nature of illness.

35
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:36,880
how the patient in question transitioned from normal human to patient, i.e. what happened to

36
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:42,980
them to prompt our arrival on scene. After that is number of patients. We want to avoid missing

37
00:04:42,980 --> 00:04:48,220
patients by focusing only on the first thing we see on scene. Then we consider the need for

38
00:04:48,220 --> 00:04:53,460
additional resources. This could be more ambulances, air medical, fire and rescue,

39
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:59,660
law enforcement, technical rescue, whatever might be necessary. All of these things are subject to

40
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:04,640
change as new information becomes available, but the thought here is to think about them all early

41
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:09,560
on before we get distracted by patient care. And then lastly, we want to consider the need for

42
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:14,320
cervical spine precautions, whether that be immobilization for those stuck in the past

43
00:05:14,320 --> 00:05:21,420
or spinal motion restriction for the cool kids. After the scene size up or scene survey is the

44
00:05:21,420 --> 00:05:26,340
primary assessment, primary survey, or initial assessment. All these names refer to the same

45
00:05:26,340 --> 00:05:31,880
thing. First step is to form a general impression as we enter the scene and lay eyes on the patient.

46
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:38,340
This is often described as the initial sick or not sick determination and is based solely on how

47
00:05:38,340 --> 00:05:44,120
the patient looks at first sight. This is similar to the idea of the across the room assessment for

48
00:05:44,120 --> 00:05:49,400
pediatric referred to as the pediatric assessment triangle in PALS. We'll come back to that idea

49
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:55,240
later on. After that, we introduce ourselves and determine a level of responsiveness or

50
00:05:55,240 --> 00:06:00,560
consciousness. Next, it's to figure out what is going on with the patient by either asking them

51
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:05,240
or looking for immediately life-threatening injuries. And then we move into the classic

52
00:06:05,240 --> 00:06:12,580
ABCs, airway, breathing, and circulation. Airway entails opening the airway if needed and placing

53
00:06:12,580 --> 00:06:19,040
an NPA or OPA as indicated. Breathing includes assessing breathing, ensuring adequate ventilation,

54
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:24,680
providing oxygen, and then fixing things that might obstruct breathing, such as decompressing

55
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:30,340
attention pneumothorax. And then circulation involves three assessments, checking a pulse,

56
00:06:30,780 --> 00:06:35,120
rate, rhythm, and quality, checking the skin, color, temperature, and condition,

57
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:40,480
and performing a blood sweep. The blood sweep is where we expose the patient and check for

58
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:45,160
life-threatening bleeds that we missed earlier on in the assessment. And if noted, we treat those

59
00:06:45,160 --> 00:06:52,220
immediately. Circulation also includes treating for shock. The specifics of that vary depending

60
00:06:52,220 --> 00:06:57,520
on the level of the provider, but at a minimum, we teach to keep the patient warm. And then the

61
00:06:57,520 --> 00:07:02,520
last step in the primary is to determine a transport priority. This is essentially deciding

62
00:07:02,520 --> 00:07:06,720
if the patient needs to get moving as soon as possible or if there's time to complete an

63
00:07:06,720 --> 00:07:13,340
assessment and perform interventions on scene prior to transport. Next on the NREMT assessment

64
00:07:13,340 --> 00:07:19,180
for trauma patient is to get vital signs and obtain what we call a sample history. Vital signs

65
00:07:19,180 --> 00:07:23,800
should be familiar to everyone listening, SAMPLE is a mnemonic to walk us through specific things

66
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:29,980
we want to know about a patient's. It stands for signs and symptoms, allergies, medications,

67
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:37,340
past medical history, last oral intake, and events leading up to the incident. It's a simple way for

68
00:07:37,340 --> 00:07:41,720
us to gather some basic need-to-know information about a patient's medical history and what is

69
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:47,060
currently going on. This may or may not be possible to obtain, but per the assessment checklist,

70
00:07:47,060 --> 00:07:50,840
we at least make an attempt to do so or acknowledge that it isn't possible.

71
00:07:52,740 --> 00:07:57,380
The final area of focus in the trauma assessment is the head-to-toe or secondary assessment.

72
00:07:57,860 --> 00:08:02,760
This might be done on scene or it might be done in transport. There are also cases where it never

73
00:08:02,760 --> 00:08:06,860
gets done because we get caught up with managing life threats noted in the primary survey.

74
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:12,360
But in testing and in practice, we do work through all the steps, so we'll give a quick overview here.

75
00:08:13,120 --> 00:08:17,520
We won't get into all the details, and again, it may make more sense to check out one of

76
00:08:17,520 --> 00:08:21,440
the linked videos, but this step basically includes getting our hands on the patient,

77
00:08:21,740 --> 00:08:25,960
starting at the head, working down the body to the extremities, and then the back as we

78
00:08:25,960 --> 00:08:26,500
roll the patient.

79
00:08:26,980 --> 00:08:32,120
The idea is to systematically assess each part of the patient's body, looking for specific

80
00:08:32,120 --> 00:08:33,480
things in each region.

81
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:40,160
So for example, when we assess the head, the specific steps include inspecting the head,

82
00:08:40,580 --> 00:08:45,900
palpating the skull, assessing the pupils for reactivity. Some folks may also include things

83
00:08:45,900 --> 00:08:50,600
like peeking in the eyes and nose and looking into the mouth again, which we already did when

84
00:08:50,600 --> 00:08:55,620
we assessed airway. After that, we'd move on to the neck, checking to make sure the trachea is

85
00:08:55,620 --> 00:09:01,720
midline, assessing for JVD or jugular vein distension, palpating the cervical spine at

86
00:09:01,720 --> 00:09:07,600
the back of the neck. Then we'll move on to the chest, abdomen, pelvis, etc. on down the body.

87
00:09:08,500 --> 00:09:13,740
During this secondary assessment, if we come across life-threatening injuries, we definitely

88
00:09:13,740 --> 00:09:15,540
stop and treat those as they are found.

89
00:09:16,100 --> 00:09:19,720
For everything else, however, we defer treatment until the end.

90
00:09:20,520 --> 00:09:24,740
Things like dressings for superficial bleeding or splints can wait until the assessment is

91
00:09:24,740 --> 00:09:27,080
done and may even get deferred altogether.

92
00:09:27,680 --> 00:09:32,860
The idea here is to identify abnormal findings and injuries and to establish a baseline to

93
00:09:32,860 --> 00:09:35,440
which we can compare subsequent findings on assessment.

94
00:09:36,300 --> 00:09:43,460
So we work our way down the body, head to foot, go back and assess the arms, then roll the patient over and do the back.

95
00:09:44,100 --> 00:09:49,080
After that, we give treatments and perform interventions as we have time and as is indicated.

96
00:09:49,820 --> 00:09:55,360
And that's how we do what we do, according to the NREMT and the skill sheets most of us learned on.

97
00:09:56,020 --> 00:09:57,600
A few notes on all of this.

98
00:09:58,120 --> 00:10:03,400
First is that there is one step about reassessing the patient either after interventions or in route.

99
00:10:03,760 --> 00:10:04,700
Don't forget to do that.

100
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:13,380
Second, the value in a system like this is that it forces the user to look for specific, important things at or in specific parts of the body.

101
00:10:13,940 --> 00:10:16,740
But that whole approach hinges on sticking to the system.

102
00:10:17,200 --> 00:10:19,840
If we deviate from the system, we miss things.

103
00:10:20,340 --> 00:10:24,760
So either stick to the system or come up with a better system, which we'll discuss in just a few.

104
00:10:25,520 --> 00:10:28,060
Third, go back to the top if you get lost.

105
00:10:28,060 --> 00:10:33,920
You can always start back at the top with how does the patient look and the ABCs and then carry on from there.

106
00:10:33,920 --> 00:10:37,040
That's a totally acceptable and an encouraged strategy.

107
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:45,260
Now, this design for patient assessment does seem to work best with the unresponsive trauma patient,

108
00:10:45,580 --> 00:10:46,920
so that's why we started there.

109
00:10:47,360 --> 00:10:50,880
It gets a little bit weird when you have a patient with an isolated injury

110
00:10:50,880 --> 00:10:53,320
who is both reliable and participatory.

111
00:10:54,120 --> 00:10:56,340
It's also a bit funky with medical cases,

112
00:10:56,600 --> 00:11:00,680
as the history-gathering and focused assessment make the system less rigid.

113
00:11:01,300 --> 00:11:06,140
The idea there is that it may not make sense, and it may be a bit awkward to work through

114
00:11:06,140 --> 00:11:08,400
a head-to-toe assessment on a patient who doesn't need it.

115
00:11:09,160 --> 00:11:13,860
That decision comes with experience, and some outfits or programs teach this concept differently.

116
00:11:14,400 --> 00:11:18,760
But if you're not sure, just stick with the system and do a complete assessment as outlined

117
00:11:18,760 --> 00:11:19,420
in the skill sheet.

118
00:11:19,960 --> 00:11:22,080
And that's often the way we teach it at basic levels.

119
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:27,240
Perform the head-to-toe on everyone in order to dial in the technique and ensure that nothing

120
00:11:27,240 --> 00:11:27,800
gets missed.

121
00:11:27,800 --> 00:11:34,160
For the sake of illustrating this idea, let's consider a patient who presents with an isolated leg injury.

122
00:11:34,640 --> 00:11:37,800
The initial steps of the whole process are the exact same.

123
00:11:38,340 --> 00:11:39,640
Start with a scene size-up.

124
00:11:40,100 --> 00:11:47,740
Scene safety, BSI, mechanism of injury, number of patients, additional resources, cervical spine precautions.

125
00:11:48,480 --> 00:11:50,700
Then the primary survey or initial assessment.

126
00:11:51,500 --> 00:11:57,640
General impression, level of consciousness, chief complaint, ABCs, transport decision.

127
00:11:57,800 --> 00:12:03,080
baseline vitals and history are the same as for the unresponsive patient except

128
00:12:03,080 --> 00:12:07,040
that we'll actually be able to collect on it the next bit is where things

129
00:12:07,040 --> 00:12:11,420
deviate from the plan performing a complete secondary or head-to-toe

130
00:12:11,420 --> 00:12:15,080
assessment on someone who is alert and oriented when there's no suspicion of

131
00:12:15,080 --> 00:12:19,880
other injuries may not be warranted in that case we can focus in on the region

132
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:23,660
of the body affected if it's the leg that's injured we need to get eyes and

133
00:12:23,660 --> 00:12:29,720
hands on there. Expose, look, touch, and assess for specific things like pulses, motor function,

134
00:12:29,780 --> 00:12:35,080
and sensation, just as you would in a full head-to-toe. If we decide that things aren't as

135
00:12:35,080 --> 00:12:40,060
expected and there is more going on than initially thought, we can start back at the top or perform

136
00:12:40,060 --> 00:12:45,180
that head-to-toe at any time. Now, the medical patient is also less straightforward than the

137
00:12:45,180 --> 00:12:50,340
unresponsive trauma patient we started with, but in a different sort of way. With the truly sick

138
00:12:50,340 --> 00:12:55,920
or unresponsive medical patient, we start with the scene size up and the primary, and then proceed

139
00:12:55,920 --> 00:13:00,460
to a head-to-toe or secondary assessment that goes systematically from superior to inferior.

140
00:13:01,080 --> 00:13:06,240
The only difference here is that it might matter a bit more whether or not we start with the head-to-toe

141
00:13:06,240 --> 00:13:12,020
or whether we start by gathering a history to figure out the backstory. It is important to note

142
00:13:12,020 --> 00:13:18,080
that this decision comes after the primary survey or initial assessment where we hopefully identify

143
00:13:18,080 --> 00:13:23,700
immediate life threats. That bit always comes first. But at the point after we've done that

144
00:13:23,700 --> 00:13:28,800
primary survey, the decision is whether to move on to a history gathering series of questions

145
00:13:28,800 --> 00:13:34,880
or perform a detailed assessment. One rule of thumb here is that we do a head-to-toe first

146
00:13:34,880 --> 00:13:39,680
on the trauma patient and then proceed to the history, and that we gather a history first on

147
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:44,740
the medical patient and then proceed to do a head-to-toe afterwards. You can think of this

148
00:13:44,740 --> 00:13:50,060
as an assessment triangle, as is taught quite often in the outdoor medicine community. At the

149
00:13:50,060 --> 00:13:55,740
top is the primary, on one side is the head to toe or physical, and on the other is the history.

150
00:13:56,200 --> 00:14:01,740
The trauma pathway takes us around the triangle in one direction, from primary to physical to

151
00:14:01,740 --> 00:14:06,980
history, and the medical pathway goes in the opposite direction, from primary to history to

152
00:14:06,980 --> 00:14:11,840
physical. If that's tough to follow, we'll add a graphic in the show notes that might make it a bit

153
00:14:11,840 --> 00:14:17,640
clearer. And then if we have a more stable medical patient that doesn't warrant a complete head-to-toe

154
00:14:17,640 --> 00:14:23,160
or secondary, we can perform a focused assessment. A good practice is to always get hands on the

155
00:14:23,160 --> 00:14:27,000
patient, even if it seems like we can figure out the story from a well-gathered history.

156
00:14:27,900 --> 00:14:33,180
And then the very last thing to mention about assessment in EMS is how we do the history

157
00:14:33,180 --> 00:14:40,480
gathering bit. This includes two components, sample and OPQRST. Sample, just to review since

158
00:14:40,480 --> 00:14:47,160
we mentioned this before, stands for signs and symptoms, allergies, medications, past medical

159
00:14:47,160 --> 00:14:52,540
history, last oral intake, and events leading up to the incident. It's something we teach that

160
00:14:52,540 --> 00:15:00,020
needs to be gathered for all patients if possible. OPQRST stands for onset, provocation or palliation,

161
00:15:00,660 --> 00:15:07,500
quality, radiation, severity, and time. We use that to delve into the details when someone is

162
00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:14,560
complaining of pain. So for example, the series of questions might go like this. Onset. How did

163
00:15:14,560 --> 00:15:21,000
the pain start? Was there a precipitating event? Was it gradual or sudden? Provocation and palliation.

164
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:28,340
Does anything make it better? Does anything make it worse? Quality. Can you describe the pain for me?

165
00:15:29,500 --> 00:15:36,280
Radiation. Do you feel it anywhere else? Severity. On a scale from 0 to 10, 10 being getting your

166
00:15:36,280 --> 00:15:41,660
arms ripped off by Tyrannosaurus Rex and Zero being chilling on the couch watching TV, how bad

167
00:15:41,660 --> 00:15:50,220
is your pain? And time, when did the pain begin? OPQRST can also be used to get details of a non-pain

168
00:15:50,220 --> 00:15:54,860
complaint, and there are also other memory tools for specific complaints and patient presentations,

169
00:15:55,220 --> 00:16:01,880
but we won't get into all those details here. So to summarize how we do assessment in EMS,

170
00:16:01,880 --> 00:16:09,000
The system starts the same for all patients. Scene size-up includes scene safety, BSI,

171
00:16:09,800 --> 00:16:15,460
mechanism of injury or nature of illness, number of patients, need for additional resources,

172
00:16:16,080 --> 00:16:22,440
and consideration of cervical spine precautions. Primary survey or initial assessment includes

173
00:16:22,440 --> 00:16:28,380
general impression, level of consciousness or level of responsiveness, chief complaint or life

174
00:16:28,380 --> 00:16:35,440
threats, ABCs, and transport priority. We then move to either a physical exam in the case of trauma

175
00:16:35,440 --> 00:16:41,080
or a history with medical patients. After whichever of those, we close the loop and do the history

176
00:16:41,080 --> 00:16:46,300
gathering for the trauma patient or the physical exam for the medical patient. And it's up to our

177
00:16:46,300 --> 00:16:51,340
discretion whether or not a complete head-to-toe is warranted or if a focused exam is sufficient.

178
00:16:51,340 --> 00:16:58,080
Patient assessment in nursing, on the other hand, is taught in a few different ways.

179
00:16:58,720 --> 00:17:02,620
There are courses within the nursing field that do teach algorithm-based assessments

180
00:17:02,620 --> 00:17:07,900
similar to how we do in EMS, but those tend to be specialized in the field of emergency medicine.

181
00:17:08,440 --> 00:17:14,300
The best example of this is TNCC with their extended ABC's algorithm to guide assessment

182
00:17:14,300 --> 00:17:19,580
and initial steps in resuscitation. We'll add a link to an overview of that for those of you

183
00:17:19,580 --> 00:17:24,680
who are curious to know what that's all about. But aside from specific courses, nursing tends

184
00:17:24,680 --> 00:17:30,100
to teach assessment via model much more focused on either primary care or acute in-hospital care.

185
00:17:30,660 --> 00:17:34,920
There are many different ways that assessment is taught in nursing, but one that is quite common

186
00:17:34,920 --> 00:17:41,180
and of value to us in the CCT world is a systems-based approach. A systems-based approach

187
00:17:41,180 --> 00:17:46,940
to assessment focuses on each body system one at a time in an effort to understand what is going on

188
00:17:46,940 --> 00:17:52,580
with a patient. This framework for assessment isn't commonly discussed in EMS, but is likely

189
00:17:52,580 --> 00:17:57,300
familiar to anyone who has worked in acute care in the hospital. It's often how we structure

190
00:17:57,300 --> 00:18:03,400
handover reports between shifts and units, especially in the ICU. A partial example of

191
00:18:03,400 --> 00:18:10,340
a handoff in an ICU might sound something like this. We have Mr. Smith in 209, neuro. He's

192
00:18:10,340 --> 00:18:14,500
sedated on propofol with fentanyl for pain, occasionally gets agitated with movements or

193
00:18:14,500 --> 00:18:19,740
turning. Wrist restraints in place. He does withdraw from pain and opens eyes to stimuli,

194
00:18:19,900 --> 00:18:26,900
but does not track. Pupils equal in reactive. Respiratory. 7.5 ET tube in place, 23 centimeters

195
00:18:26,900 --> 00:18:35,120
at the teeth. ACVC at 18 and 450. 40%, 5 a peep. Synchronous at rest. Sometimes triggers with

196
00:18:35,120 --> 00:18:42,020
movement. Lung sound clear. Chest x-ray looks good. Hope to extubate soon. GI. Decreased bowel

197
00:18:42,020 --> 00:18:45,980
sounds but did have his first bowel movement last night, with small aim we're continuing with his

198
00:18:45,980 --> 00:18:53,340
regimen, GU, fully in place, clear yellow urine, responds well to his Lasix, and on and on. No need

199
00:18:53,340 --> 00:18:58,960
for us to continue with all this as you probably get the idea. We can then take this same idea

200
00:18:58,960 --> 00:19:04,480
and apply it to assessment at the bedside. This is particularly useful during inter-facility

201
00:19:04,480 --> 00:19:09,280
transports when we don't have the urgent need of immediately assessing a new situation

202
00:19:09,280 --> 00:19:12,440
for which the EMS system we discussed is designed.

203
00:19:12,940 --> 00:19:17,600
It can also help on scene runs when somebody else has been looking after the patient for a while

204
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:19,900
and already has things more or less sorted.

