00:20:38,200 --> 00:20:43,160
The idea is that the systems-based approach is useful for ongoing management or accepting a

221
00:20:43,160 --> 00:20:47,920
handover from another crew or staff. The algorithm-based approach we teach in EMS

222
00:20:47,920 --> 00:20:52,080
is well-suited for initial encounters or acutely decompensating patients.

223
00:20:52,900 --> 00:20:57,960
Another thing we can work into the systems-based approach is management of all the machines and

224
00:20:57,960 --> 00:21:02,100
devices. This is particularly useful when picking up a patient from an ICU.

225
00:21:02,920 --> 00:21:05,920
Some folks prefer to address this stuff after an assessment.

226
00:21:06,560 --> 00:21:08,400
Some opt to work them into the assessment.

227
00:21:09,140 --> 00:21:12,200
Others tend to divvy up the machine or device management and patient assessment

228
00:21:12,200 --> 00:21:14,500
between partners in a two-human crew.

229
00:21:15,060 --> 00:21:18,600
That's totally okay to do, but does lend itself to oversight.

230
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:22,660
Just be sure that things like drug doses on pumps, vent settings,

231
00:21:23,140 --> 00:21:24,560
output in containers, etc.

232
00:21:24,900 --> 00:21:28,540
are all incorporated into our understanding of what is going on with the patient.

233
00:21:28,540 --> 00:21:33,820
and if it helps to address those things during review of systems, have at it.

234
00:21:35,380 --> 00:21:41,200
Just to summarize up to this point, we've got the algorithm-based assessment from our fine folks in EMS

235
00:21:41,200 --> 00:21:45,040
that works well for initial encounters or acute decompensation

236
00:21:45,040 --> 00:21:51,840
and a systems-based approach from the nursing friends for ongoing assessment or transfer of care from one provider to another.

237
00:21:52,640 --> 00:21:57,460
This delineation, while not set in stone and definitely not to be taken as the one and only

238
00:21:57,460 --> 00:22:02,120
way to approach patient assessment in critical care transport, may help make sense of things

239
00:22:02,120 --> 00:22:03,320
for some listeners out there.

240
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:07,940
No one system is going to meet every need, but this does seem to work pretty well.

